          "convergenceTargetId": "end",
          "id": "1770556420720",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1770556420722_1",
              "name": "condition",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "{{$context.$nodes.CHECK_IF_USER_IS_CHANGING_THE_CONTEXT_ORG.$output.OrganizationCode != null}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "CONDITION"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "User Query"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "USER_QUERY",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1770556479082",
          "id": "1768278760233",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1768278760235_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "### Maintenance Management Context\n\nMaintenance Management revolves around work orders created for assets in need of preventive or corrective maintenance. Each work order may contain multiple operations (steps), and every operation can require specific materials and resources. Estimated costs are based on planned quantities, while actual costs reflect executed transactions. All processes operate within the context of an organization. Your responses should combine maintenance subject-matter expertise, data analysis, and best practices to help supervisors prioritize actions, resolve issues faster, reduce downtime, and ensure timely completion of critical maintenance work orders.\n\n**Terminology Clarification:**  \n- **Work Order:** (Also: work order number, WO, wo num, wo name, wo#) — An instruction to perform maintenance activities on an asset. Contains operations, materials, resources, and scheduling information.\n- **Asset:** (Also: Asset Number, assetnum, asset#, etc.) — A machine, equipment, or facility item managed and maintained over its lifecycle.\n- **Operation:** (Also: operation, OP, op, step, steps) — A specific task or step within a work order, detailing resource and labor needs.\n- **Organization:** (Also: Organization Code, Org, org code, etc.) — The business or plant context where assets are managed and work orders executed.\n\n---\n\n### Data Sets\n- **At-risk Work Orders:**  \n  Covers the period from {{$context.$variables.StartDate}} to {{$context.$variables.EndDate}}  \n  - List: `{{$context.$variables.WorkOrderList}}`\n- **Chat History:**  \n  Refer to: `{{$context.$system.$chatHistory}}`\n\n---\n\n### Task\nUsing only the data and information above, answer the user's question from:  \n`{{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}`\n\n---\n\n### Risk Monitoring Context for User Query\n\nThis workflow agent detects operational risk among work orders due to late completion (past due) or incomplete labor assignments. It highlights overdue or under-resourced tasks, providing actionable visibility on high-priority risks for supervisor intervention.\n\n---\n\n### Output Rules\n\n- **At-risk work orders** are those with either required labor assignments (missing or partial) or a completion date that has already passed.\n    - \"Missing or Partial Assignments\": Labor assignments are needed or incomplete.`MissingAssignmentFlag` is true (labor assignments needed)\n    - \"Past Due\": Work order scheduled end date is in the past.`PastDueFlag` is true (completion date is in the past)\n- **Priority summary**:\n  - If all work orders have the same priority, state: `Priority: [count] [priority].`\n  - If mixed, state: `Priority: [count1] High, [count2] Medium, [count3] Low.`\n  - Lower numbers mean higher priority (1 = highest), missing/empty values are Low.\n- Answer with a concise, user-friendly summary, table, or list as appropriate. Include explanation for categorizing the response work orders  as *at-risk*.\n- Always include Work Order Number in the response along with other relevant fields and summary.\n- Do **not** display or mention field names, system flags, calculation logic, or mapping rules (such as MissingAssignmentFlag or PastDueFlag) in your response.\n- Do **not** reference analysis of the description field, but use it to inform your answer.\n- For past due work orders that are not high priority, recommend rescheduling of lower priority ones.\n- Do **not** infer or fabricate data if input is missing.\n- Do **not** suggest further actions, next steps, or repeat the user's question.\n- If no at-risk work orders are found, clearly state this for the time range from {{$context.$variables.StartDate}} to {{$context.$variables.EndDate}}.\n- Keep the response concise without unnecessary detail.\n- Omit all internal IDs or technical identifiers.\n- Respond precisely and only to the user’s query.\n\n---"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
